outputs.exact_statement:
If Theorem <a href="#thm:main" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="thm:main">1</a> has a counterexample, then there is a finite nonempty oriented graph $G$ in which every vertex has positive indegree and $$\label{eq:strict-subset-growth}
  \lvert U\rvert+\lvert F_G^2(U)\rvert<2\lvert F_G(U)\rvert
  \qquad\text{for every }\varnothing\ne U\subsetneq V(G).$$

Let $D$ be a finite nonempty oriented graph such that $\lvert N_1^+(v)\rvert>\lvert N_2^+(v)\rvert$ for every $v\in V(D)$. Choose $D$ with minimum order and, subject to that, minimum number of arcs among all graphs with this property. Then $D$ is strongly connected, every vertex has positive indegree, and $$\label{eq:subset-deficit}
  \lvert F^2(S)\setminus F(S)\rvert<\lvert F(S)\setminus S\rvert
  \qquad\text{for every }\varnothing\ne S\subsetneq V(D).$$

There is no nonempty finite oriented graph $G$ in which every vertex has an in-neighbor and whose one-step image operator $$F(U)=\{y\in V(G):x\to y\text{ for some }x\in U\}$$ satisfies $$\label{eq:impossible-concavity}
 \lvert U\rvert+\lvert F^2(U)\rvert<2\lvert F(U)\rvert
 \qquad(\varnothing\ne U\subsetneq V(G)),$$ where $F^2=F\circ F$.
